---
/* The owner's editor. Injected by `editorRoute()` — see ../astro/index.ts for
   why this lives in the kit rather than in six site repos.

   Wiring only. Every control, every string and every rule about what an owner
   may change is @shaahink/sitekit/editor. CLAUDE.md's "no design system in the
   kit" rule governs the pages a visitor sees; this is not one of those. It is
   the same argument the lift made in 0.7.0, carried one file further.

   Its own route, on purpose. Google Identity Services loads a script from
   accounts.google.com, opens an iframe there, talks to it, and injects its own
   stylesheet — and putting all of that in the site's global CSP would loosen
   the hash-strict posture on every public page to serve a page almost nobody
   visits. Astro emits the policy per page, so the looser directives attach
   here and nowhere else, and the public pages stay untouched by construction
   rather than by a path rule somebody has to keep correct.

   Deliberately not wrapped in the site's layout: no nav, no footer, no
   review-mode gate, and on a site with islands, none of them. Nothing on this
   route needs the site's framework and nothing pulls it in.

   The `lang` below is a starting value, not the answer, and that comment was
   amended in 0.17.0. It used to say the panel's chrome was English because a
   tool's language is not the site's — true until session 17 drove the editor
   on nimagiti's Farsi page and elfine's French one and found it saying "Tap
   any highlighted text to change it." to both owners. The kit ships three
   locales now, and one /edit route serves both halves of a bilingual site, so
   there is no site page to read a language off: `mountEditor` resolves one
   (?lang= → what it remembered → the browser's own preference) and writes both
   `lang` and `dir` onto this document. A static attribute stays because a
   document needs one before any script runs, and the panel's stylesheet is
   entirely logical properties, so `dir` is the whole of the right-to-left
   layout. The fields keep their own dir="auto" regardless — a Farsi entry in
   an English panel was always meant to read right-to-left. */

/* The panel's stylesheet is copied into public/ by the site's `npm run editor`
   (the kit ships the copier; CI regenerates and diffs it) and linked below
   rather than imported. Astro collects every processed stylesheet's hash into
   every page's style-src, so importing it here would add the editor's hash to
   the CSP of every public page — harmless, since it only ever permits a style
   whose bytes match that file, but it would mean the public policy is no
   longer provably untouched. A static asset under 'self' costs nothing and
   keeps that guarantee exact.

   In frontmatter rather than beside the <link>, because Astro passes HTML
   comments through to the built page and reasoning is not something to ship
   to an owner's browser. */

import config from "virtual:sitekit-editor-route";

/* Optional because a site may run without CSP at all. The hand-written pages
   asserted it with `!` because every site in the fleet had it switched on;
   site twenty might not, and a missing policy should mean a working editor
   with no policy rather than a build that fails to render. */
const csp = Astro.csp;

if (csp) {
  csp.insertScriptResource("https://accounts.google.com");

  /* Only what the editor itself loads: Google's iframe, the site's own images
     and the in-browser preview the picker draws as a data: URI, the avatar
     Google serves from lh3, and its own /api handlers under 'self'.

     These merge into whatever the site already declares — insertDirective
     appends, it does not replace, which is the opposite of what the six pages
     this replaces believed. So nothing here has to restate a site's public
     sources, and nothing here can take one away. */
  csp.insertDirective("frame-src https://accounts.google.com");
  csp.insertDirective("connect-src 'self' https://accounts.google.com");
  csp.insertDirective("img-src 'self' data: https://lh3.googleusercontent.com");

  /* Naming any element-kind style source moves this page from `style-src` to
     `style-src-elem`, and that directive does not inherit `style-src`'s
     sources — so 'self' has to be restated or the panel's own stylesheet is
     blocked on the one page that needs it.

     The third line is the one that cost four commits in four repos on
     2026-07-28. Google Identity Services does load a stylesheet into this
     document, and 'unsafe-inline' never covered it: a host is not inline, and
     CSP ignores 'unsafe-inline' the moment a hash is present, which Astro's
     own stylesheet hashes guarantee. The origin has to be named.

     And the second line is not redundant with it, which is what 0.16.0
     corrected here: GIS *also* injects a 9.7 kB <style> element at runtime, so
     'unsafe-inline' is load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces — and on a site
     whose build contributes hashes to this directive it was being ignored, in
     silence, exactly as the sentence above says it would be. Measured on
     sk-works against elfine, same kit, three hashes against none. Those hashes
     belong to other pages and the fix is in style-policy.ts, because nothing
     visible from inside a single page's frontmatter can see them. */
  csp.insertStyleResource({ resource: "'self'", kind: "element" });
  csp.insertStyleResource({ resource: "'unsafe-inline'", kind: "element" });
  csp.insertStyleResource({ resource: "https://accounts.google.com", kind: "element" });

  /* And the style *attributes* Google sets on the elements it injects. Hashes
     never apply to attributes, so 'unsafe-inline' is the only spelling that
     works.

     Emitted only where the site does not already declare it. Not for
     correctness — measured on nimagiti, emitting it anyway yields
     `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-inline'`, which every browser
     reads the same way. It is for identity: this route replaces six
     hand-written pages across four client sites, and "the built page is
     byte-for-byte what it was" is a claim worth being able to make without
     reviewing each one. A duplicated token would cost that for nothing. */
  if (config.needsStyleAttr) {
    csp.insertStyleResource({ resource: "'unsafe-inline'", kind: "attribute" });
  }
}
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>{config.title}</title>
    <!-- Nothing here belongs in an index, and there is no public content on
         this page to find anyway. -->
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/editor-panel.css" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <main data-sk-editor>
      <!-- The panel builds itself from the content schemas; without
           JavaScript there is nothing to show and nothing to promise. -->
      <noscript>
        <p>The editor needs JavaScript. Everything else on this site doesn't.</p>
      </noscript>
    </main>

    <script>
      import { mountEditor } from "@shaahink/sitekit/editor";

      const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-sk-editor]");
      if (root) mountEditor(root);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
